From 92a3b4c5d6e7f8091a2b3c4d5e6f70819200220 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Claude Fable 5.1" <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 9 Sep 2026 09:10:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 22/22] mmc: retry a failed block read before giving up

mmc_bread() treats one failed transfer as a failed read of the whole request.
Everything above it -- the filesystem drivers, the environment, the boot
scripts -- treats a failed read as a failed boot, so a single transient costs
the board its operating system.

A failed transfer is not evidence that the medium is bad. sdhci_send_command()
already resets the command and data lines on its way out, so the next attempt
starts from a clean controller, and the card is still there. Attempt each
chunk up to three times, waiting for the card to report itself ready in
between, and only then report the failure. The warning names the device and
the LBA so a retry that succeeds is still visible in the console log rather
than being silently absorbed.

Measured on an Axera AX630C (Sipeed NanoKVM-Pro) reading a 48.8 MiB kernel as
100k single-block commands, where the residual failure rate of one transfer in
some tens of thousands was otherwise fatal to the boot.

Signed-off-by: open-nanokvm-pro contributors <nanokvm@localhost>
---
 drivers/mmc/mmc.c | 34 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/mmc/mmc.c b/drivers/mmc/mmc.c
index 8888888..9999999 100644
--- a/drivers/mmc/mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/mmc.c
@@ -455,6 +455,19 @@
 	return mmc_send_cmd(mmc, &cmd, NULL);
 }
 
+/*
+ * How many times a read is attempted before the block layer calls it a failure,
+ * and how long the card is given to become ready between attempts.
+ *
+ * A data transfer that fails is not evidence that the medium is bad: the host
+ * controller resets its command and data lines on the way out, so the next
+ * attempt starts from a clean state. Giving up on the first one turns any
+ * transient into a failed boot, which on a board whose only storage is this
+ * card is the difference between a delay and a brick.
+ */
+#define MMC_READ_RETRIES		3
+#define MMC_READ_RETRY_BUSY_MS		100
+
 static int mmc_read_blocks(struct mmc *mmc, void *dst, lbaint_t start,
 			   lbaint_t blkcnt)
 {
@@ -549,10 +562,27 @@
 	b_max = mmc_get_b_max(mmc, dst, blkcnt);
 
 	do {
+		int tries = MMC_READ_RETRIES;
+
 		cur = (blocks_todo > b_max) ? b_max : blocks_todo;
-		if (mmc_read_blocks(mmc, dst, start, cur) != cur) {
-			pr_debug("%s: Failed to read blocks\n", __func__);
-			return 0;
+		while (mmc_read_blocks(mmc, dst, start, cur) != cur) {
+			if (--tries <= 0) {
+				pr_debug("%s: Failed to read blocks\n",
+					 __func__);
+				return 0;
+			}
+
+			/*
+			 * The transfer failed and the host driver has reset the
+			 * command and data lines, but the card may still be
+			 * finishing what it was asked to do. Wait for it to
+			 * report itself ready before asking again; the result
+			 * does not matter, the retry itself is the test.
+			 */
+			mmc_poll_for_busy(mmc, MMC_READ_RETRY_BUSY_MS);
+
+			log_warning("mmc%d: retrying read at " LBAF "\n",
+				    dev_num, start);
 		}
 		blocks_todo -= cur;
 		start += cur;
-- 
